ism: Not excluded but requires further endocrine investigation"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional symptoms (weight loss, abdominal pain)", "Cognitive changes (trouble focusing)", "Hypercalcemia (Ca2+ 12.5 mg/dL)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Malignancy with Hypercalcemia", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.13580255149113557, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Severe hypercalcemia in a non-smoker with constitutional symptoms (weight loss, cognitive changes) but normal physical findings is more characteristic of malig…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "multiple myeloma", "squamous cell carcinoma", "primary hyperparathyroidism"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Hyperparathyroidism", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2376046690087649, "posterior": 0.6869649873636813, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Elevated alkaline phosphatase is a known bone turnover marker in hyperparathyroidism, with an LR+ of 2.0. This provides moderate support for hyperparathyroidis…"], "evidence_against": ["Severe hypercalcemia in a non-smoker with constitutional symptoms (weight loss, cognitive changes) but normal physical findings is more characteristic of malig…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Drug-induced Hypercalcemia (Antacid Overuse)", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.002, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Vitamin D Excess (Toxicity/Granulomatous Disease)", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.002, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Other Hypercalcemic Disorders", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.174, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Hematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Multiple Myeloma)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.06035668955161581, "posterior": 0.06035668955161581, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "multiple myeloma", "squamous cell carcinoma", "primary hyperparathyroidism"]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Solid Tumor with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Squamous Cell Carcinoma)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04526751716371185, "posterior": 0.04526751716371185, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Humoral Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HHM) with PTHrP Overproduction", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.030178344775807903, "posterior": 0.030178344775807903, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "multiple myeloma", "squamous cell carcinoma", "primary hyperparathyroidism"]}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of constitutional symptoms (weight loss, cognitive changes) in a non-smoker with normal exam findings argue against a hematologic malignancy as the cause of hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Constitutional symptoms in a non-smoker with normal exam findings are consistent with hematologic malignancy like lymphoma or multiple myeloma but lack definitive support for exclusion."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Other Hypercalcemic Disorders? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "The severe hypercalcemia (12.5 mg/dL) is most inconsistent with antacid-induced hypercalcemia, which rarely exceeds 12 mg/dL, but supports malignancy-related hypercalcemia."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the severe hypercalcemia (12.5 mg/dL) with constitutional symptoms (weight loss, cognitive changes) but normal physical exam findings strongly favor malignancy over hyperparathyroidism as the ca…", "summary": "Severe hypercalcemia in a non-smoker with constitutional symptoms (weight loss, cognitive changes) but normal physical findings is more characteristic of malignancy (e.g., lymphoma, myeloma) than primary hyperparathyroidism, which often presents with bone pain or renal stones. This moderately suppo…"}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated alkaline phosphatase (150 U/L) in the context of hypercalcemia support primary hyperparathyroidism via bone turnover markers?", "summary": "Elevated alkaline phosphatase is a known bone turnover marker in hyperparathyroidism, with an LR+ of 2.0. This provides moderate support for hyperparathyroidism but is less specific than other findings."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of bone pain or palpable lymphadenopathy in the physical exam contradict malignancy-related hypercalcemia?", "summary": "While malignancy-related hypercalcemia can present without bone pain or lymphadenopathy (e.g., lymphoma or myeloma with systemic involvement), the absence of these findings is slightly less supportive but not conclusive. This results in a neutral effect."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the low urinary sodium (5 mmol/24h) inconsistent with primary hyperparathyroidism's typical urinary calcium findings?", "summary": "Low urinary sodium is not a standard discriminative feature for hyperparathyroidism. Hyperparathyroidism typically presents with hypercalciuria, but this finding is not directly addressed by the evidence. The effect remains neutral."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B5", 0.392], ["B1", 0.298], ["B2", 0.266]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.265], ["B2.1", 0.251], ["B1", 0.155]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.687], ["B5", 0.174], ["B1", 0.136]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability markers are present. Patient is normotensive (115/71 mmHg), normothermic (37°C), and has normal respiratory rate (12/min). No signs of shock, airway compromise, or severe neurological deterioration.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "No branch has posterior ≥0.75. Dangerous alternatives (B1 with posterior 0.136 and danger 0.8) remain unresolved despite B2 being the leading hypothesis (posterior 0.687). Info_exhaustion not triggered as askable_discriminators like PTH levels and cancer biomarkers have not been requested yet."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.38, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.6869649873636813, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation of this patient's current presentation?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Antacid overuse"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Increased parathyroid hormone"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Malignancy"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Viral illness"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Abnormal circulating calcium concentration', 'High blood calcium levels', 'Hypercalcemia', 'Hyponatremia', 'hyperparathyroidism', 'pica'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B5", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.392}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Hyperparathyroidism", "posterior": 0.687, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 1}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Severe hypercalcemia in a non-smoker with constitutional symptoms (weight loss, cognitive changes) but normal physical …"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B5", "B1.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=both]\n\nHyperparathyroidism vs Hematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Multiple Myeloma):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, autosomal dominant inheritance, depression, frequent urination, kidney stones\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nHyperparathyroidism vs Humoral Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HHM) with PTHrP Overproduction:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, autosomal dominant inheritance, bone pain, constipation, depression\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nHematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Multiple Myeloma) vs Humoral Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HHM) with PTHrP Overproduction:\n  Favours first:  altered mental status, bone pain, confusion, constipation, decreased appetite\n\nRelated to Hyperparathyroidism: familial isolated hyperparathyroidism, familial primary hyperparathyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, transient neonatal\n\nRelated to Hematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Multiple Myeloma): hypercalcemia disease, paraneoplastic syndrome\n\nRelated to Humoral Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HHM) with PTHrP Overproduction: hypercalcemia disease, paraneoplastic syndrome\n\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.50):]\n  Source: Hyperparathyroidism. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Malignancy-Related Hypercalcemia\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: Hyperparathyroidism. > Treatment / Management\n  Medical Management and Surveillance\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.64):]\n  Source: Hyperparathyroidism. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Malignancy-Related Hypercalcemia\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.72):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Introduction\n  More than 90% of hypercalcemia cases are caused by primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT) or malignancy-induced hypercalcemia, with malignancy being the most common cause of hypercalcemia in hospitalized \n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  abdominal pain and recent weight loss → malignancy-related hypercalcemia → Hematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Multiple Myeloma)\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Investigate for underlying malignancy (e.g., multiple myeloma) given abdominal pain, weight loss, and hypercalcemia in this African American patient\n  abdominal pain and trouble focusing → hypercalcemia-induced neurological dysfunction → Humoral Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HHM) with PTHrP Overproduction\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess serum calcium levels to confirm hypercalcemia, which would support HHM or other malignancy-related hypercalcemia\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
